, independent ITS-90 fixed point 1357.77 K (tier1.json, NIST): crystal grew at 1200 and 1300 K, melted at 1400 and 1500 K -> MACE-MP-0 Tm(Cu) in (1300,1400) K, consistent with experiment. RESULT: MACE-MP-0 Tm(Al) in (825, 900) K; linear interpolation of the energy-drift zero gives ~884 K, inside the bracket. The measured Al melting point is 933.45 K (NIST-JANAF, tier1.json), i.e. this model predicts a melting point roughly 35-110 K too low (~4-12%); the experimental value lies outside the simulated bracket, so the discrepancy is attributed to the potential (PBE-trained foundation model, not fit to Al liquid thermodynamics), not to sampling. For the physical answer use 933.45 K; this number is the MACE-MP-0 prediction. UNCERTAINTY (+/-38 K) covers only the temperature-grid bracketing resolution; it does NOT cover the model (DFT/MLIP) error, finite-size effects (512 atoms, ~16 A interfaces separation), Berendsen barostat artefacts, float32 precision, or stochastic interface fluctuations (one run per T). The 900 K run had only a small positive drift, so Tm(MACE) is probably in the upper part of the bracket (~870-895 K).\"\n },\n \"investigation_id\": \"\",\n \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-01T01:10:33.223422+00:00\",\n \"content_hash\": \"91085857b096803e17d9048c139584c7975c8337193e29e110f583e930df99b5\",\n \"parent_artifact_ids\": [\n  \"1df3c4c4-704d-4125-b6a4-ff8ddd799e4f\"\n ],\n \"result_quality\": \"ok\",\n \"needs\": [],\n \"summary\": \"aluminum melting_point = 862 K via ASE 3 + mace-torch (cuEquivariance, float32) on NVIDIA GB10/MACE-MP-0 medium (foundation MLIP, PBE/MPtrj-trained) Solid-liquid coexistence (interface direction) method. 4x4x8 fcc supercell (512 atoms) from MACE-relaxed a0=4.039 A; solid equilibrated by anisotropic Berendsen NPT at 900 K (1.5 ps); top half melted with Langevin at 2250 K (2 ps) while bottom half fixed, then 0.5 ps at 900 K; coexistence runs at 825/900/975/1050 K with anisotropic Berendsen NPT (P=0, taut=100 fs, taup=500 fs), 7 ps each. 